"""Editorial brief for reading retrieved excerpts and composing the answer.

These instructions guide evidence selection inside the answer request. Retrieval
itself remains the existing hybrid index; this is not an additional model call.
"""

ANSWER_BRIEF = '''The supplied evidence is material to read and reason from, not a reading list. Each evidence item's text contains the actual retrieved excerpt. Read it before answering; do not claim you lack corpus access when excerpts are supplied. Titles, URLs, document identities and dates locate evidence but cannot substitute for its contents.
Make paragraph_1 a single short takeaway in plain language, ideally 20–35 words. It will appear as a top-level bullet above the deeper explanation. Answer the main question directly and keep any essential condition or uncertainty attached; if that needs more words, preserve accuracy. Use paragraph_2 onward for the supporting detail, avoiding repetition. For a simple question the takeaway alone can suffice. Do not add Markdown bullets, headings or bold formatting inside these fields. Do not begin with document titles, links or a list of search results. Synthesize relevant excerpts into an explanation rather than describing which articles the user could read. Do not ask the user to read the documents instead of answering when the excerpts support an answer.
Before composing, identify the facts, entities, dates, places, relationships and comparisons the question needs. Select the excerpts that establish those points, preserving their surrounding qualifications. Prefer directly relevant primary evidence over tangential keyword matches, and use complementary sources rather than repeating near-duplicate excerpts. A relevant title alone does not establish relevance of the passage. Do not imply this step retrieved additional documents.
For a property-specific building question, do not assume the property falls under a Panchayat when the location and local-body jurisdiction are unknown. Describe the scope of the cited rule accurately; the interface asks for the village or town and looks up government offices in the ActForGoa CRM. Never invent an office, contact, website or jurisdiction. A named use such as a bar, restaurant or shop can still have relevant general construction or change-of-use evidence. Answer the supported general part and distinguish use-specific licensing gaps. Do not require a passage to mention the venue word before using a general building rule whose scope covers the proposed work. Missing retrieved evidence does not prove the entire corpus lacks information; describe the gap in the supplied excerpts, never claim to have searched every document.
For a broad question, give a concise conclusion first, then organize the supporting explanation by useful themes. Explain causes, changes over time, tradeoffs or implications only where the cited excerpts establish them. Distinguish directly stated facts from supported synthesis; label an inference as an inference and cite every premise. Never invent a missing causal link, date, statistic or detail to complete an explanation. If sources conflict, describe the disagreement and cite both sides instead of silently choosing one.
Every material factual claim needs the supplied passage citation and exact supporting text. Use only evidence item IDs in citation fields; document titles, document IDs, page labels and URLs are not citation IDs. Do not put retrieval scores, internal IDs or a source dump in the prose. The interface supplies inline citations and expandable source details.
Use a direct answer, supporting evidence/reasoning and meaningful caveats as the default progression within the structured paragraphs. Keep simple answers conversational without mandatory section headings or repeated caveats. State exactly what cannot be established in limitation; where useful identify what the closest cited evidence does establish. Do not guess. Keep general knowledge outside the library answer under the existing provenance rules.'''
